        "2.1 People do not respond to utility alone\nUtility matters. People want tasks to be easier, faster, safer, cheaper, or more reliable. But human attention is also drawn to meaning, emotion, identity, novelty, and narrative.",
        "Consider ordinary experiences:\n- People want a government service to be efficient, but they also care about whether the process feels confusing or disrespectful.\n- Customers want a meal delivered correctly, but they also care about reliability and the overall experience.\n- People may choose a familiar product even when another option is technically similar because the familiar brand carries trust or meaning.\n- A sports match becomes compelling when the result is uncertain and the stakes feel important.\nThe functional result is only part of the experience.",
        "2.2 What makes a story compelling?\nIn this framework, storytelling is broader than writing fiction. It is the structure through which people understand change.",
        "A useful narrative often contains:\n1. A starting situation: What is happening now?\n2. A tension or problem: What is missing, threatened, difficult, or unresolved?\n3. A desired outcome: What would be better?\n4. A path: What action or transformation could bridge the gap?\n5. A result: What changes if the path works?\nThis structure appears in a product pitch, a job interview, a landing page, a presentation, a social post, or a personal conversation.",
        "2.3 The basic value narrative\nA simple structure is:",
