      "id": "chapter-19",
      "number": 19,
      "title": "Understand People, Then Build Something Valuable",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Conclusion",
        "The next few years will bring new tools, platforms, business models, and technical capabilities. Some skills will become more important; others will change. It is difficult to predict every development in advance.",
        "But the ability to understand people remains useful because people are involved in nearly every meaningful exchange of value.",
        "The framework in this playbook gives you a way to practise that ability:\n- Understand the eight broad desires that can shape attention.\n- Use the three tensions—survival, identity, and progress—to understand why a message matters.\n- Apply the five levers—name the threat, mirror the identity, exclude carefully, paint the transformation, and give the first step—to communicate value.\n- Test your understanding through writing, speaking, building, selling, and real conversations.\n- Use feedback to improve rather than treating persuasion as a collection of tricks.\nThe most important distinction is between manipulating attention and creating value.",
        "Manipulation tries to make someone act in your interest regardless of whether the action helps them. Ethical persuasion helps someone understand a real problem, evaluate a relevant solution, and choose a useful next step.",
        "Do not become someone who only knows how to make people pay attention. Become someone who understands what people need and can create something genuinely worth their attention.",
        "Learn the people. Learn the problem. Create the value. Communicate it clearly. Then improve through feedback."
      ]
